   359	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   360	# THE IMPLEMENTED ASSERTIONS. One entry per CHECKABLE clause id in the pin; the
   361	# bijection between this dict's keys and the pin's CHECKABLE ids is predicate
   362	# (b) and is enforced in BOTH directions below.
   363	#
   364	# Probe kinds:
   365	#   ("present", path, regex)          regex must match at least once in path
   366	#   ("min",     path, regex, n)       regex must match at least n times in path
   367	#   ("absent_tree", tree, regex, exts) regex must not match in any text file
   368	#                                      under tree (exts=None ⇒ every text file)
   369	#   ("enum_exact", path, ty, members) the Rust `enum ty` in path has EXACTLY
   370	#                                      these variants — set AND count
   371	#   ("arms_exact", path, ty, fn, pairs) the match arms inside `impl ty { fn fn }`
   372	#                                      map EXACTLY these (variant, string) pairs
   373	#
   374	# THERE IS NO FILE-SCOPED NEGATIVE PROBE KIND, DELIBERATELY (fix-3). See "PROBE
   375	# SCOPE" in this file's header: every NEGATIVE assertion is `absent_tree`, and
   376	# `("absent", <file>, regex)` was DELETED rather than merely left unused, so
   377	# re-introducing a single-file negative is a deliberate, reviewed act.
   378	#
   379	# ------------ CLOSED VOCABULARIES ARE STRUCTURAL, NOT BLACKLISTED ------------
   380	# fix-1, codex §9 round 1 finding #1 [P2]. Three clauses below assert that a
   381	# vocabulary is EXACTLY some set: readiness is "EXACTLY {ready, embedding}",
   382	# roles are "exactly {filterable, rankable, searchable}", the typed id space is
   383	# "total over exactly those three". Those obligations were originally probed as
   384	# "the members I want are PRESENT" plus, in one case, "one specific bad name is
   385	# ABSENT" — a BLACKLIST OF ONE. A blacklist is not a closed vocabulary: any
   386	# member added under a name nobody thought to forbid walks straight through, and
   387	# the gate reports 0 on a tree that violates the contract. codex demonstrated it
   388	# with `pub enum DenseReadiness { Ready, Embedding, Failed }`.
   389	#
   390	# `enum_exact` / `arms_exact` close that by CONSTRUCTION: they parse the enum
   391	# body and the match arms, count the members, and compare the member SET against
   392	# the pinned one. A third variant fails whatever it is called; a third accepted
   393	# string spelling fails even when the enum is untouched. That is the difference
   394	# between "the names I feared are absent" and "the vocabulary is closed".
   395	#
   396	# ------------------ SQL IS CASE-INSENSITIVE (AND WHITESPACE) -----------------
   397	# fix-1, codex §9 round 1 finding #2 [P2]. An `absent` probe over SQL that
   398	# anchors on ONE uppercase spelling with single spaces is a false negative:
   399	# SQLite accepts `insert into t`, `INSERT OR REPLACE INTO t`, `INSERT OR IGNORE
   400	# INTO   t` and a newline anywhere whitespace is legal. codex cleared the gate
   401	# with a single lowercase line. Every negative-space probe whose subject is SQL
   402	# now goes through `insert_into()` / an explicit `(?i)` pattern that tolerates
   403	# the five SQLite conflict clauses and arbitrary whitespace.
   404	#
   405	# Rust IDENTIFIER probes (`ProjectionRole::Vector`, `EntityTypeSpec`,
   406	# `id_prefix`) are deliberately left case-SENSITIVE: case is significant in Rust,
   407	# so folding case there would create false positives, not close a hole.
   408	#
   409	# ----------------- A SQL TABLE NAME IS `[<schema>.]<table>` ------------------
   410	# fix-2, codex §9 round 2 findings #1 and #2 [P2]. fix-1 widened these probes for
   411	# CASE and WHITESPACE but still required the pinned table name IMMEDIATELY after
   412	# `INTO` / `TABLE`. That is not how SQLite names a table: every table reference
   413	# may carry a schema qualifier, and `main.` is ALWAYS valid for the main
   414	# database. `INSERT INTO main.canonical_attributes ...` therefore writes exactly
   415	# the forbidden table and cleared the gate with 0; so did `CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE
   416	# main.property_search_index ...`. `qualified()` below tolerates one optional
   417	# schema identifier — quoted, bracketed or bare, with whitespace around the dot,
   418	# which SQLite also accepts.
   419	#
   420	# NOTE WHAT THIS DOES **NOT** REACH: an ATTACHed alias whose NAME is chosen at
   421	# runtime, or a table reached through a view/trigger under a different name, is
   422	# still invisible here — see RESIDUAL SCOPE in this file's header. `qualified()`
   423	# closes the spellings of the PINNED name, not every route to the pinned table.
   424	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   425	
   426	# One SQL identifier, optionally quoted `"x"` / 'x' / `x` / [x].
   427	_SQL_IDENT_OPEN = r"[\"'`\[]?"
   428	_SQL_IDENT_CLOSE = r"[\"'`\]]?"
   429	
   430	
   431	def qualified(table):
   432	    """`[<schema> . ]<table>` — an optional schema qualifier before the pinned
   433	    table name, each part optionally quoted/bracketed, whitespace tolerated
   434	    around the dot (all of which SQLite accepts)."""
   435	    schema = (
   436	        r"(?:" + _SQL_IDENT_OPEN + r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*" + _SQL_IDENT_CLOSE
   437	        + r"\s*\.\s*)?"
   438	    )
   439	    return schema + _SQL_IDENT_OPEN + table + r"\b"
   440	
   441	
   442	def insert_into(table):
   443	    """A case-insensitive `INSERT [OR <conflict>] INTO [<schema>.]<table>` /
   444	    `REPLACE INTO [<schema>.]<table>` pattern.
   445	
   446	    Tolerates all five SQLite conflict clauses (the same five the schema crate's
   447	    own accretion guard enumerates), arbitrary whitespace including newlines
   448	    between every token, an optional schema qualifier, and an optionally
   449	    quoted/bracketed table name.
   450	    """
   451	    return (
   452	        r"(?i)\b(?:INSERT|REPLACE)\b"
   453	        r"(?:\s+OR\s+(?:ABORT|FAIL|IGNORE|REPLACE|ROLLBACK))?"
   454	        r"\s+INTO\s+" + qualified(table)
   455	    )
