  1130	def body_strings(body):
  1131	    """Every string literal appearing anywhere in `body`.
  1132	
  1133	    The NEGATIVE half of the closed-vocabulary check (fix-2, codex §9 round 2
  1134	    finding #3). Expanding or-patterns closes ONE extra syntax; it does not close
  1135	    the general case, because a token can be admitted by syntax that never forms
  1136	    a match arm at all — an `if value == "pending" { return Some(..) }` guard
  1137	    before the match, a `matches!`, a `starts_with`. Rather than enumerate those
  1138	    forms (an open set — the same asymptote this gate has now been asked to chase
  1139	    twice), the check is inverted: inside a function whose whole job is to map a
  1140	    CLOSED vocabulary, EVERY string literal must be one of the pinned spellings.
  1141	
  1142	    Consequence, stated plainly: a legitimate non-vocabulary literal added to one
  1143	    of these three tiny functions (an error message, say) turns the gate RED. It
  1144	    is a false RED — the safe side of a publish-precondition gate, and the same
  1145	    trade already documented for the SQL text probes. What it is NOT is the
  1146	    silent green it replaces.
  1147	    """
  1148	    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(_STRING_CAP, body)]
